dhcp-oru-toolkit (2.1.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Fix Kea lease files that exist but are not readable being reported as
    "not found".  On a stock Ubuntu Kea install /var/lib/kea and its lease
    files are owned by _kea and unreadable by anyone else, so every
    unprivileged run looked like an empty DHCP server instead of a missing
    sudo.  The 2.1.0 LFC support probed candidate files with Path.exists(),
    which raises PermissionError on Python 3.12 and older and silently
    returns False on 3.13 and newer -- a traceback on one and a misleading
    "not found" on the other.  Lease files are now probed with an explicit
    stat: only a genuine ENOENT counts as absent, and an unreadable file
    stays in the candidate list so the read reports the real reason.
  * Report permission failures once per address family, naming the directory
    and saying to re-run with sudo, instead of once per LFC generation (the
    generations cannot be enumerated when the directory is unsearchable, so
    listing them individually invented files that may not exist).
  * Report an unreadable Kea config (/etc/kea is 0750 on a stock install)
    instead of silently falling back to the default lease path.

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 17:30:00 +0000

dhcp-oru-toolkit (2.1.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * dhcp-lease-list: make Kea DHCP a first-class server rather than a
    hardcoded pair of CSV paths. Kea was nominally supported since 1.3.0, but
    on a real Kea host the viewer routinely showed nothing, stale state, or
    leases with no MAC:
      - --server now defaults to "auto" and detects what is installed, so a
        bare "dhcp-lease-list" works on a Kea-only host. An explicitly named
        lease file is identified by sniffing its format, so --v4-lease/
        --v6-lease no longer have to be paired with --server. "both" reports
        ISC and Kea side by side; "isc" and "kea" still force one server.
      - Kea lease files are located from "lease-database": { "name": ... } in
        /etc/kea/kea-dhcp4.conf / kea-dhcp6.conf instead of assuming
        /var/lib/kea/kea-leases{4,6}.csv. The Kea config dialect (//, # and
        /* */ comments, <?include?> directives) is pre-processed before
        parsing. Added --kea-config-dir to point at a non-standard location.
      - A MySQL or PostgreSQL lease backend, or a memfile with
        "persist": false, is now reported as such instead of printing an
        empty table with no explanation.
      - Lease File Cleanup generations (<name>.1, <name>.2, <name>.completed)
        are read alongside the primary lease file, in Kea's own load order.
        While a cleanup is in flight -- or permanently, if LFC was interrupted
        -- most leases live in those files, and reading only the primary file
        under-reported or missed them entirely.
      - Kea's journal is now replayed with Kea's own semantics: the last row
        for an address wins, and a row with valid_lifetime 0 deletes it. The
        previous "active always wins" rule kept released, declined and
        reclaimed addresses on display as though they were still held.
      - The MAC is recovered from the DHCPv6 DUID (DUID-LLT, DUID-LL and the
        O-RAN ASCII DUID-EN) when Kea leaves the hwaddr column empty, and from
        the DHCPv4 option 61 client-id (RFC 2132 and RFC 4361 forms) for v4.
        Previously such leases displayed "-" and could not be tied to a unit.
      - Kea expiry timestamps are rendered in UTC, matching the ISC lease
        files and the "Expires (UTC)" column heading; they were being
        converted with the server's local clock.
      - The lease start time is derived from expire - valid_lifetime, and a
        vendor class stored in the lease's user-context is displayed in the
        Vendor Class column (Kea's &#xNN; CSV escapes are decoded).
  * dhcp-lease-list: fixed lease-expiry evaluation, which compared a UTC lease
    timestamp against the local clock. On a server east of UTC this hid
    still-valid leases from the default view; west of it, expired leases
    lingered. This affected ISC leases as much as Kea ones.

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

dhcp-oru-toolkit (2.0.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Renamed package dhcp-lease-list -> dhcp-oru-toolkit, with the proper
    dpkg rename declarations so the new package cleanly supersedes the old one
    instead of colliding on /usr/local/sbin/dhcp-lease-list:
      Provides:  dhcp-lease-list (= 2.0.0)
      Conflicts: dhcp-lease-list (<< 2.0.0)
      Replaces:  dhcp-lease-list (<< 2.0.0)
    Without these, "apt install ./dhcp-oru-toolkit_2.0.0_all.deb" failed with
    "trying to overwrite '/usr/local/sbin/dhcp-lease-list', which is also in
    package dhcp-lease-list 1.3.0" whenever the 1.x package was installed.
  * Refactored the single-file lease viewer into a pure-stdlib Python package
    (dhcp_toolkit) installed under /usr/local/lib/dhcp-oru-toolkit.
  * Ship TWO tools to /usr/local/sbin via thin python3 wrappers:
      - dhcp-lease-list (ISC + Kea, IPv4 + IPv6) with a new --conflicts mode.
      - dhcp-forensics: pcap/DHCP analyzer for the O-RU shared-xid defect.
  * Packaging fixes:
      - postinst/prerm now back up and restore the SAME path they install to
        (/usr/local/sbin); the 1.x scripts backed up /usr/sbin while
        installing to /usr/local/sbin.
      - Dropped the questionable "Replaces: isc-dhcp-server" control field.
      - postinst no longer clobbers its own wrapper when re-run.
  * Bumped Depends to python3 (>= 3.8).

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Tue, 02 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000

dhcp-lease-list (1.3.0) stable; urgency=low

  * Added Kea DHCP CSV lease file support (kea-leases4.csv, kea-leases6.csv)
  * Added --server {isc,kea} switch to select DHCP server type
  * Kea state codes mapped: 0=active, 1=declined, 2=expired, 3=released
  * Kea journal-aware dedup: last active entry per IP address wins
  * Added declined/released to --state filter options

